To resolve this, we'll create a new configuration file specifically for your application and link it to the `sites-enabled` directory. This approach avoids modifying the default configuration file directly.

1. **Create a new configuration file for your application**:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/api-forward
```

Add the following content to the file:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;

    server_name your_domain_or_ip;

    location /api {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:5000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }

    location / {
        # Other configurations for your front-end
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }
}
```

Replace `your_domain_or_ip` with your actual domain name or IP address.

2. **Enable the new configuration by linking it to the `sites-enabled` directory**:

```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/api-forward /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
```

3. **Test the Nginx configuration**:

```bash
sudo nginx -t
```

4. **Reload Nginx to apply the changes**:

```bash
sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

Now, Nginx should forward all requests to `/api` to your local app running on port 5000. Make sure your app is running and listening on port 5000. You can verify this by running:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:5000
```

This command should return the response from your local app. If everything is set up correctly, your Nginx server will proxy requests to the appropriate backend service based on the URL path.
